      "math_academy_id": "q-129138",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}[\\frac{1}{3}f(t)+2g(t)]\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,6,\\,3⟩$\nand \n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,-1⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{3}[\\frac{1}{3}f(t)+2g(t)]\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+2{\\int }_{-1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}[\\frac{1}{3}f(t)+2g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+2{\\int }_{-1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-3,\\,6,\\,3⟩+2⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩+⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,4,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
